  "id": "Compound::DB00831 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Trifluoperazine is linked to Chrm2 through a series of molecular interactions that span multiple pharmacological classes. The evidence suggests that Trifluoperazine, as a member of the phenothiazine class, binds to Chrm2 via a shared pathway involving prochlorperazine and mesoridazine. This is supported by the lowest common ancestor (LCA) relationships, which indicate that Trifluoperazine and its derivatives share a common ancestry within the phenothiazine family. The binding of these compounds to Chrm2 appears to be a conserved mechanism, as multiple phenothiazines, including prochlorperazine and mesoridazine, are known to interact with the same receptor. The presence of a causal link between Trifluoperazine and Parkinsonian rest tremor, along with its binding to Chrm2, further reinforces the role of this receptor in the pharmacological effects of the drug. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Trifluoperazine exerts its effects, with Chrm2 serving as a key target in the molecular network governing its action.",
